      "problem": "Solve the inequality $3-y>2y+5.$",
      "worked_solution": "We solve the inequality just like we would solve an equation.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-y & >2y+5 \\\\ 3-y-2y & >2y+5-2y \\\\ 3-3y & >5 \\\\ 3-3y-3 & >5-3 \\\\ -3y & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the multiplication principle.\n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3y & >2 \\\\ \\frac{-3y}{-3} & \\,\\,<\\,\\,\\frac{2}{-3} \\\\ y & <-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
